# @boundary runs
#
# THE ONE-ELEMENT INFERRED LIST LITERAL. Found by mutation testing (B1), 2026-08-01 —
# the first survivor the harness ever produced, and it points at a real hole rather
# than at a quirk of the harness.
#
# The surviving mutation was `selfhost/CodeGen.zbr:9714`:
#
#     else if ll.elems.len > 0 and .infer_ctx != nil     ->     ll.elems.len > 1
#
# That is the branch that infers a list literal's element type from `elems.at(0)` when
# there is NO annotation to take it from. Changing `> 0` to `> 1` disables it for
# exactly one input shape — a literal with a single element — and every gate stayed
# green.
#
# WHY: corpus-wide, across test/, examples/, selfhost/ and IDE/, there are ZERO
# one-element inferred list literals and three multi-element ones. A mutation whose only
# observable effect is at length 1 therefore cannot be observed. The sibling probe
# bv_list_literal_annotated.zbr does contain `var one: List(int) = [7]`, which looks like
# coverage and is not: the annotation sends it down the *other* branch, where the element
# type comes from the annotation and inference is never consulted.
#
# EXPECTATIONS BELOW WERE WRITTEN FROM QUICKSTART BEFORE RUNNING THIS FILE, as the A3
# discipline requires. `[7]` is a list of one int; its element type is int because 7 is an
# int, by the same rule that makes `[7, 8]` a list of int. Nothing about the count enters
# into it.
def main()
    # The failing shape, minimally: one element, no annotation.
    var one = [7]
    print(one.count())
    print(one.at(0))

    # The element type must come from the element, not from a default. If inference is
    # skipped, the emitted element type falls back to a string-shaped default and this
    # row is where that shows up as arithmetic on the wrong type.
    var n = one.at(0) + 1
    print(n)

    # A single STRING element — a different Zig element type, so a fallback that happens
    # to be string-shaped would pass the int rows above and fail here only if the two are
    # checked together. Both are checked.
    var s = ["hi"]
    print(s.at(0))
    print(s.count())

    # Length one is the boundary; its neighbours must keep working. Zero elements has no
    # element to infer from and is covered by bv_empty_list.zbr; two is the smallest case
    # the corpus already exercises.
    var two = [7, 8]
    print(two.count())

    # Still mutable afterwards, as with the annotated form.
    var m = [1]
    m.add(2)
    print(m.count())
